        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Life Fitness T3 worth the price?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For serious, frequent runners who want genuine commercial-gym
              reliability at home, yes. The commercial-grade AC motor and
              fixed-frame construction cost significantly more than
              home-brand alternatives, but they deliver a level of
              durability and consistency under heavy daily use that
              DC-motor home treadmills generally can't match. Casual or
              occasional users likely won't get proportional value from the
              premium.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the Life Fitness T3 use an AC motor instead of a DC motor?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              AC motors are what actual commercial gyms use because they
              tolerate sustained, heavy daily use far better than the DC
              motors found in most home treadmills. DC motors are cheaper to
              produce and fine for moderate home use, but they generate more
              heat and wear faster under continuous high-mileage running.
              Life Fitness builds the T3 with the same commercial-grade AC
              motor technology used in its gym equipment, which is the core
              reason it costs more than home-brand competitors.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Life Fitness T3 fold for storage?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. The T3 uses a fixed-frame, commercial-style construction
              rather than a folding design. This is a deliberate trade-off:
              fixed frames are inherently more rigid and stable under
              sustained heavy use than folding frames, but the T3 requires
              a permanent dedicated footprint and is heavier and harder to
              move than a comparable home-brand folding treadmill.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a subscription to use the Life Fitness T3?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. The T3 is available with the basic Go Console or with
              Track Connect app integration, and neither requires an
              ongoing subscription to access core treadmill functionality.
              Track Connect adds workout tracking and entertainment app
              access for those who want it, but speed, incline, and
              built-in programs all work fully without it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the Life Fitness T3 compare to home treadmill brands like NordicTrack?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The T3's core advantage is its commercial-grade AC motor and
              fixed-frame construction, both derived from actual gym
              equipment, versus the DC motors and folding frames typical of
              home brands like NordicTrack. Home brands often win on
              touchscreen size and content ecosystems for a lower price; the
              T3 wins on raw mechanical durability and the higher 400-lb
              weight capacity, at a meaningfully higher cost.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who should buy the Life Fitness T3 instead of a cheaper home treadmill?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Frequent runners who use a treadmill nearly every day,
              households with multiple heavy users, or anyone who has worn
              out a DC-motor home treadmill and wants genuine gym-grade
              reliability this time. Casual walkers or occasional users are
              unlikely to see proportional value and should look at
              home-brand alternatives instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
